Rust model and tree entry points Use master::model for the canonical network collection and master::treefor integer search. This example needs the clp feature: use baldes::{Graph, graph::Window, master::{model::{self, Network, Problem}, tree}};

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    let mut graph = Graph::<2>::new(3, 1)?;
    for vertex in 0..3 {
        graph.set_bounds(vertex, 0, 0., 10.)?;
    }
    graph.add_arc(0, 1, -2.)?;
    graph.add_arc(1, 2, 0.)?;
    let problem = Problem {
        rows: vec![],
        networks: vec![Network {
            id: 7, graph, multiplicity: Window::new(0., 1.)?,
            arc_entries: vec![vec![], vec![]], route_entries: vec![], activity_entries: vec![],
        }],
    };
    let root = model::solve(problem.clone(), model::Options::default())?;
    assert_eq!(root.master.lp, -2.);
    let integer = tree::solve(problem, tree::ModelTreeOptions::default())?;
    assert_eq!(integer.incumbent, -2.);
    Ok(())
}
 Problem::routing(graph, vehicles) produces a RoutingContext with routingregistration metadata. master::routing::solve runs its root relaxation;tree::solve(context, TreeOptions::default()) uses the same tree driver.See network models, routing treeand architecture for the registration-specific contracts. This is a breaking modernization. The old routing/network command aliases,separate Python solve functions, native solve exports, and Rust master::rootand master::mp paths are removed. Use master::routing, master::model,and the common master construction and search modules. Master construction, dynamic-row insertion, artificial columns, pricing, andtree selection/evaluation dispatch are shared. Model hooks supply coefficients,cut families, branch candidates, witnesses and certification. Different modelpolicies can still select different LP bases or search trajectories; scalarversus array shape does not select a different policy.

Node lifecycle Output<D>::snapshot owns the solved master, generated-column registry, rowmaps and one independent pricing solver per network. Its methods borrow theparent and return a new child or solve result; solving either child leaves theparent and sibling unchanged. Oracle counters in each output describe thatsolve, while column tags stay stable across descendants. snapshot.solve(options) resumes all networks with a new work budget. snapshot.with_row(row, coefficients) appends a shared additive row.RowCoefficients identifies a network, gives one coefficient per originalphysical arc and a once-per-route coefficient. Omitted networks contributezero. Retained master columns, the generated pool and future pricing all usethe row. Callers are responsible for the validity of supplied cuts. snapshot.fractional_assignments(tolerance) aggregates each network's use ofunit-cover shared rows. Assignment membership comes from row coefficients,so matching local arc or vertex IDs in different networks cannot alias. snapshot.branch_assignment(shared_row, network_id, assigned) builds theuse >= 1 or use <= 0 child. Assignment rows must be unit equalities withnonnegative integer contributions. Both children price every enabled network. snapshot.fix_route(route_index, quantity) fixes additional integer copiesof a registered original-feasible elementary route. It replays resources,subtracts every shared/branch/cut and multiplicity contribution, and carriesthe cost as an objective offset. Rows appended later also subtract this fixedcontribution. Output.fixed_routes reports the fixed portion separately;Output.values contains only remaining quantities. For example, after obtaining a coupled root: let candidate = root.snapshot.fractional_assignments(1e-6)?[0];
let assigned = root.snapshot.branch_assignment(
    candidate.shared_row, candidate.network, true,
)?;
let excluded = root.snapshot.branch_assignment(
    candidate.shared_row, candidate.network, false,
)?;
let assigned_result = assigned.solve(options.clone())?;
let excluded_result = excluded.solve(options.clone())?;
let restored_parent = root.snapshot.solve(options)?;
 Fresh roots still require an empty column registry; descendants own their warmmetadata internally. Pass pricing options through Options.pricing_options.Scalar resource capacity is a const generic, defaulting to two; seescalar resources.

Policy controls and registered original variables stabilization_max_tree_depth uses the recovered signed native boundary:activation requires actual branch depth strictly below the limit (default10000). A zero or negative limit disables smoothing/proximal participation fromthe root. Resumes and ordinary row additions preserve depth. The correspondingCLI flag is --stabilization-max-tree-depth. network_promising_limit exposes the independent native positive-status budget(default 1000000), alongside network_unpromising_limit (default 1). A normalmodern oracle still has status zero, even when it produces columns. Negativereduced cost does not imply a promising status. Register original integer aggregate variables explicitly when their meaningspans networks, route constants or static integer master variables: Arc coefficients may be integers or exact half-integers. Half-integer mappingsrequire a graph parity proof that every source-to-sink walk contributes aninteger value, including walks with repeated arcs. This supports splitting avertex assignment equally across its incoming and outgoing arcs. Per-route andstatic-master coefficients still require integers. result = problem.solve(
    tree=True,
    mapped_variables=({
        "name": "total-route-count",
        "priority": 1.,
        "networks": [
            {"network": 7, "per_route": 1.},
            {"network": 42, "per_route": 1.},
        ],
    },),
)
 Each network contribution also accepts sparse arcs: [[arc_id, coefficient], ...];activities: [[descriptor_index, coefficient], ...] adds intrinsiccustom/cumulative activities. Activity multipliers must be bounded integers,and each descriptor needs a proof of exact integer activity on every path.Integer initial levels, increments and weights admit a linear-time programproof on DAGs and explicitly bounded walks when all intermediate levels,products and accumulated charges stay within the exact integer range (2^53).Other cases use directed intrinsic enclosures and numerical replay over acomplete bounded path scan; incomplete domains are rejected.masters: [[variable_id, coefficient], ...] adds static integer variables.Coefficients must be bounded integers. Names identify generators; they do notinfer membership. MappedBranchVariable::from_shared_row derives selectednetwork/master contributions of a known original packing or linking row.Branches install floor/ceiling bounds through the shared-row lifecycle, includingfixed routes and future columns. Registered candidates support phased restricted-LP, heuristic-pricing and exactpricing trials, using the routing strong-branching controls. Trial objectiveestimates and history rank candidates; estimates do not become proof bounds.Trial solutions that pass original-model integer witness validation can improvethe global incumbent, including solutions found in restricted-master trials.Their objective is recomputed from original route and master-variable costs.Set mapped_strong_branching=False to select by priority/fractionality alone.Branch scores use the numerical parent bound even in certified mode. Theircontinuous-objective cutoff is the parent incumbent minus halfnetwork_scoring_gap_tolerance (default 1e-6), matching the native absolute-gapscoring margin. This setting affects scores, not published proof bounds or thetree's stopping tolerance. Rust uses scoring_gap_tolerance inModelTreeOptions; CLI uses --network-scoring-gap-tolerance.Trial witnesses update the tree incumbent after the evaluation pass returns;they do not replace the parent cutoff during the same pass.Results expose strong_branching_trials, mapped_history_ranked andmapped_branch_evaluations. The CLI accepts --mapped-variables FILE and--no-mapped-strong-branching; seemapped_variables.json. Recovered explicit proximal defaults are available throughproximal={"explicit": {}} (three-piece), or{"explicit": {"five_piece": True}}. These derive initial widths fromthe first positive ordinary-row mean when Kappa is positive, retain nativestored-float controls, and use the original row RHS for private artificialscaling. Existing bands configurations remain explicit overrides. Rustconstructors are explicit_three_piece(), explicit_five_piece() andexplicit_multipoint(). CLI equivalents are --proximal-explicit-three-piece,--proximal-explicit-five-piece and --proximal-explicit-multipoint. Unstabilized network pricing admits near-zero arc costs using the recovered native1e-6 sign precision only when a finite walk bound proves no improving path.Negative bound corrections are retained. An unavailable pricing subgradientremains unavailable, and a weak aggregate estimate falls back to real pricing.Cyclic NG relaxations without a hard walk cap keep their pricing calls. Model trees retain original-model integer witnesses during restricted-master solves.When a valid numerical or certified bound closes the witness's incumbent gap,the node can finish before another pricing pass; this is integer-domain pruning,not an LP-pricing convergence claim. Retained witnesses survive later masterchanges and are revalidated before publication. Root LP solves keep this behaviordisabled by default; Rust Options.integer_primal_termination enables it explicitly. network_branching="phased" admits original resource-group, assignment,integer master-variable, route-count, arc and time-window proposals into the samephased evaluator as registered aggregates. Family priorities can be supplied innetwork_branch_priorities; defaults are the routing adapter preset, ratherthan a claim that all native models register the same priorities. Route-countgenerators do not enter retained native-style ranking history. Rust callers canalso select completed-subtree estimates through StrongBranchingOptions.Named generators can select upper-first or lower-first throughnetwork_branch_directions, for example {"count42": "lower-first"} for aregistered aggregate called count42, or {"ESA_7_1": "lower-first"} for aregistered packing assignment. The Rust field isModelTreeOptions.branch_directions; CLI reads the same JSON map with--network-branch-directions FILE, and Python accepts the map as a keyword.Directions require phased branching (also selected by packingregistration) and a tree solve. A name can remain dormant until its generatorbecomes fractional. Direction controls both trial construction and committedchild admission; it does not override the frontier's depth/bound comparisons.Phased proposals default to upper first. Recorded side zero alwaysmeans the lower branch and side one the upper branch, regardless of executionorder; their history distances and proof outputs remain attached to those sides.Mapped activity evaluation retains signed cancellation and multiplicationresiduals when combining route copies, fixed routes and integer master terms.The resulting LP activity selects candidates and scores trials; it does notreplace an independently published proof bound.Trials use independent children. Surviving candidates keep their private LP,columns and pricing workspaces between phases. Each priced phase restores thefull parent schedule before applying its own pricing floor, so a heuristicslice cannot remove the later exact phase. Selected children can reuse completed exactpricing results and their ordinary bound provenance, including the selectedcertificate channel in certified mode. Restricted-master, heuristic andincomplete trials contribute ranking estimates and validated feasible solutions.Selected children retain those LP columns, basis and pricing workspaces evenwhen the local pass stops before exact completion. Their full pricing schedulesare restored, and ordinary tree treatment performs a fresh pricing scan.Numerical and certified lower-bound improvements published by an exact-pricingtrial survive even when its final status is incomplete. Each channel remainsindependent; heuristic and restricted-LP objectives cannot replace inheritedproof bounds. Partial estimates never become proof bounds or completed childoutputs. Discarded candidates remain isolated.Phased trials follow their generator's direction, including mapped andpacking-assignment proposals. An exact infeasible trial is retained only with anoriginal-domain phase-one proof. Its closed state participates in native queueordering and outer selection budgets, but it is neither re-priced nor countedas unresolved work. A solver status or positive artificial penalty alone doesnot authorize this closure. If the outer treatment budget is exhausted before phasedbranching starts, the current node stays unresolved without running trials.A candidate admitted before the strong-pass deadline attempts both sides. Ifthat deadline expires during its first side, the sibling receives zero remainingtime and retains its inherited proof bounds. This attempt still counts in trialand exact-processed statistics, while no LP iterations or pricing calls areinvented. A zero-iteration output preserves the preceding LP estimate, or theunknown-value sentinel for a fresh side. Set network_share_inactive_columns=true in JSON/Python, or pass--tree --network-share-inactive-columns, to share generated physical pathsbetween tree nodes and strong trials. Rust exposesModelTreeOptions.share_inactive_columns. It defaults to false. Eachrecipient rebuilds the path's schedule, original cost and row coefficients;existing route caps and enumeration universes still apply. Imported paths stayinactive until recipient repricing accepts them. Sharing does not transfer adonor's proof bounds, solved LP or pricing completion. Root-only calls rejectthe flat option because there are no other tree nodes to share with. finite_box_step() / --proximal-finite-box-step follows native functiontype1,including its finite stored-float cap1.01; the existing box_step(width) remainsa mathematical hard-band override. Public native interval names differ from thelogical band ordering: inner_half_width controls the last active pair (default10)and outer_half_width the first pair (default100). Native multipoint defaults totwo zero-width bands with stored-float caps0.9. These details are retained in the native policy evidence. Networks can declare route_domain={"kind": "bounded_walks", "max_arcs": 4}to allow repeated interior vertices within a finite arc limit. The default is{"kind": "physical_simple"}. The limit belongs to the model: pricing,certified bounds, enumeration, fixed routes and resumed children preserve it.Negative cycles may improve a bounded route, so its certified lower bound usesthe declared arc limit. A runtime pricing limit cannot silently narrow thisdomain. Direct source-to-sink arcs are valid one-arc routes when max_arcs >= 1;source/sink revisits are unsupported in this mode. Explicit vertex elementaritygroups can keep customers unique while allowing station revisits; their countmust fit the compiled NG memory capacity. Graph::add_arc_elementarity_setadds hard groups of physical arcs, limiting a route to one arc from each group.Pricing, canonical enumeration and original-route validation enforce the samegroups for bounded walks. The single-network root master supports uncuttedbounded walks. Root cut families and dynamic NG strengthening are supportedwhen the bounded-walk graph is acyclic or every interior vertex has a hardelementarity group, either of which guarantees physically simple routes;otherwise they are rejected for bounded walks.Synchronized services additionally require their member vertex to belong to adeclared hard vertex elementarity group, so revisiting a service cannot countone vehicle as two participants. front_program_domain=True is a separate, explicit model choice forcoupled-resource fronts. It defines feasibility using the computedfloating-point front program. Its independent machine checker reproduces theprogram's rounding operations; it does not prove equivalence to exact-realsource constraints. Leave the option false for the default source-domaincontract. Ambiguous source boundaries and nonfinite machine operations remainuncertified. See the retained proof-scope counterexamples. Packing-assignment branching can be registered with packing in solveoptions, or --packing FILE for JSON model input to baldes solve. For example: {"priority": 1000, "networks": [
  {"network": 7, "vertices": [[1, 1], [2, 2], [3, 0], [4, 1], [5, 2]]}
]}
 Each pair is [vertex_index, packing_index]; repeated vertex setters replaceits preceding label. Source and sink labels are rejected. Packing labels areindependent of elementarity and additive shared-row membership. Rust callersuse PackingBranching::new and NetworkPackingAssignment::new; Pythoncallers pass the same dictionary as packing=... with tree=True.Registration selects phased branching and replaces inferred shared-rowassignment proposals. Other families and their priorities remain available. Each network/group expression charges half on every incident arc endpoint;thus an arc with both endpoints in that group contributes one. Only activitiesstrictly between the tree tolerance and one minus that tolerance are admitted.Candidates prefer activities closest to one half, then pricing-configurationidentity and group index; one configuration per packing group survives beforethe ordinary strong-branching quota is applied. Configuration identity remainsstable through option clones, but allocation ties can differ across processesand from the native binary. This does not promise identical symmetric branchIDs or universal search trajectories. Explicit mappings may coexist, withnames distinct from the generated ESA_<network>_<group> names. With mapped_strong_branching=False (CLI --no-mapped-strong-branching),the packing separator is bypassed; ordinary count/original-activitybranching continues. Explicit mapped_variables retain their separaterank-without-trials behavior. Unstabilized network pricing accepts explicitly registered variable-boxbounds through Rust/JSON variable_boxes, Pythonvariable_boxes=(...), or CLI --variable-boxes FILE. For example: [{"network": 7, "variables": [{"arc": 0, "upper": 1}, {"arc": 1, "upper": 1}]}]
 Omitted variables/upper bounds remain unbounded; lower defaults to zero.These are verified pricing envelopes, not new route restrictions. A finite capmust already contain the graph's DAG/walk domain or follow from a forbidden arc.Positive lower bounds are accepted when every complete graph walk within thehard walk limit must traverse that arc. Upper-envelope checks use source/sinkconnectivity and shortest return cycles, so a cyclic graph can still prove unitcaps on bridges and tighter repeat caps within its walk limit. Bounds requiringunproved resource-specific deductions are rejected. Suppliedloose bounds retain their value; graph-derived caps do not silently replacenative inputs. Models without registered original variables retain the physical-arcorthant/near-zero policy. Negative corrections are retained, without an inventedsubgradient. A bound-only skip must close the complete current master gap oraccompany a fresh improving column; otherwise full pricing resumes. Aggregatemaster activity bounds are not per-route variable bounds. Grouped resource rowsand other omitted nonlinear terms bypass these additive admission tests. Required network multiplicity uses a bound-only correction rather than anattaining zero selection. Admission requires a closing complete master gap orfresh improving columns; an initial required route still needs a real witness.The implicit nonnegative-arc box supports this test without extra registration. Inactive-column reduced-cost scanning is opt-in. Set RustOptions::search_inactive_columns, JSON/Pythonnetwork_search_inactive_columns, or CLI --network-search-inactive-columnsto reprice retained inactive paths before invoking the pricing oracles. Thisapplies to fresh roots and resumed snapshots. Tree share_inactive_columns(JSON/Python network_share_inactive_columns) additionally transfers eligiblepaths between nodes/trials and enables scanning for the root, ordinary nodes,and strong trials. Retaining a path does not itself activate it or transfer aproof bound. With scanning disabled, ordinary pricing still discovers improvingpaths and supplies the required pricing certificates.

Solving and restricting snapshots use baldes::master::{
    model::{self, imported, MasterVariable, Options, Problem},
    preprocessing::Bounds,
    formulation::Row,
    RowSense,
};

let nonnegative = Bounds { lower: 0.0, upper: f64::INFINITY };
let result = imported::solve(
    Problem::<2> {
        rows: vec![Row { sense: RowSense::GreaterEqual, rhs: 1.0 }],
        networks: vec![],
    },
    vec![MasterVariable {
        id: 10, cost: 0.0, bounds: nonnegative, integer: false,
        entries: vec![(0, 1.0)],
    }],
    vec![imported::Subproblem {
        id: 7, variables: vec![10], multiplicity: nonnegative,
        columns: vec![imported::Column {
            id: 0, cost: 2.0, bounds: nonnegative, integer: true,
            entries: vec![(10, 1.0)],
        }],
    }],
    Options::default(),
)?;
let node = result.snapshot()?;
let fixed = node.with_column_bounds(7, 0, Bounds { lower: 1.0, upper: 1.0 })?;
let child = fixed.solve(Options::default())?;
 ImportedOutput.output contains ordinary solver status, bounds and routingresults. ImportedOutput.values.original contains the caller's originalvariable IDs and values. values.columns identifies each quantity by subproblemand local column ID. Internal master-variable IDs in low-level diagnostics arenot imported column IDs. Snapshots offer with_variable_bounds, with_column_bounds andwith_multiplicity. Restrictions intersect the parent's domain. Fixed columnskeep their full objective, original-vector and multiplicity contributions;they are not removed from the model. Parent and sibling snapshots remainindependent.

Incremental domain sources node.begin_import(subproblem_id) creates an immutable collection session forone existing group. Call session.collect(&ImportOptions, callback) to receivebatches from an external source. The callback receives the group definition,including all currently known columns, and the remaining time. ReturnColumnBatch { columns, complete }; set complete: true only when the sourcehas supplied its entire intended domain. All other groups retain their declareddomains. This protocol enumerates a domain; it supplies no reduced-cost pricingor external optimality certificate. use baldes::master::model::imported::{ColumnBatch, ImportOptions, ImportStatus};

let session = node.begin_import(7)?;
let progress = session.collect(&ImportOptions::default(), |known, remaining| {
    // Enumerate additional columns using known.columns as the authoritative
    // accepted set. Respect remaining when accessing the external source.
    Ok(ColumnBatch { columns: vec![], complete: true })
})?;
if progress.status == ImportStatus::Complete {
    let solved = progress.session.solve(Options::default())?;
}
 A batch or time limit returns a resumable progress.session. Incompletesessions reject solve and inherit no finite-parent bounds or infeasibilityconclusion. Explicit envelope-covered certificate requests are described below.Completion triggers a fresh solve with all explicit restrictions replayed.Batches are validated before acceptance; duplicate IDs, invalid vectors or asource error leave the input session usable. A failed collection call does notcommit its earlier batches. External callback side effects are not rolled back;use the supplied known columns as authoritative when retrying. Time limits arechecked between synchronous callbacks, so sources must cooperate with theremaining-time argument.

Numerical oracle replies Use tree.run_with_oracles(&tree_options, &limits, callback) when an externaloptimizer can bound its entire missing-column domain without enumerating it.The callback returns ImportOracleReply containing a ColumnBatch and anoptional ImportPricingMinimum: let minimum = point.numerical_minimum(global_reduced_cost_lower_bound)?;
Ok(ImportOracleReply {
    batch: ColumnBatch { columns: generated_columns, complete: false },
    minimum: Some(minimum),
})
 The supplied number must lower-bound the reduced cost of every permittedmissing column in that group's intended domain, at the supplied objective orfeasibility prices. A minimum over a heuristic candidate list does not satisfythis contract. An optimizer may instead bound the entire domain, including knownpatterns, which is conservative. Negative infinity means unknown; positiveinfinity declares an empty missing-column domain. Neither marks enumerationcomplete. The library checks request identity and direct contradictions withreturned columns; the external optimizer supplies the global-domain guarantee. Minima are tied to the exact request, group and phase. Reusing one from an oldersolve, another group or another phase is rejected. A round queries everyunfinished group at one stored dual point before branching. for_subproblemcan retarget a point to another unfinished group without solving another LP;its clones retain the common request identity. Columns accepted during a roundare visible in the next round; the current round keeps its original prices andknown-column prefix. In numerical bound mode, all required group minima are combined with theretained-domain bound and total multiplicity caps. Existing declared envelopescan cover a group whose reply lacks a minimum. Missing coverage or an adverseminimum with unlimited quantity keeps the bound unknown. An unavailableretained-domain bound also leaves publication unknown. A positive full-domainphase-I numerical bound can close an infeasible node under that numericaloracle contract. The result's bound_mode identifies its guarantee. In certified mode, supplied numerical minima never contribute to publication orpruning. Directed envelope certificates remain required. A resumed tree cannotchange bound mode. Plain-batch run requests no numerical minima; both methodsretain previously published bounds under the unchanged bound mode. Callback, node and time limits remain resumable. A batch limit can stop partwaythrough a round; those accepted columns persist, but minima from differentrequest epochs are never mixed on resume. Allow enough callbacks to cover allunfinished groups in one round when requesting a joint numerical bound.A round that adds no columns and completes no domain either closes or branchesthe node, or returns Incomplete with an open frontier.Errors preserve the input tree, including batches accepted earlier in thatfailed invocation; callback side effects remain caller-owned.

Optional parallel nodes Add --tree-threads N to evaluate up to N open nodes concurrently; thedefault is 1, using the sequential search. Rust callers setTreeOptions::threads. The root is evaluated first, so parallel work beginsonce branching supplies multiple open nodes. Workers receive independent parent matrices, cuts, pricing state and routeidentities. Clp basis statuses are copied; other LP backends rebuild theirinternal solver state. Native model pointers and Rc snapshots never crossthreads. The coordinator merges a bounded batch in dispatch order, updatesthe incumbent and branching history, and runs strong branching and diving.New incumbents reach workers at the next batch. Parallel search may thereforevisit more nodes or select a different optimal route set than sequential search. The node limit and deadline apply to the whole tree, including dispatchednodes. Every batch finishes before returning, and unfinished nodes retaintheir lower bounds. parallel_batches reports batches containing multiplenodes. Worker count is separate from pricing threads and --master-threads;choose those budgets together to avoid oversubscribing the machine. A complete Rust entry point, requiring clp: use baldes::{
    master::{routing::{self, RoutingContext}, tree::{self, TreeOptions}},
    inputs::{Instance, Rounding},
};
use std::time::Duration;

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    let mut instance = Instance::read("instances/Solomon/R101.txt")?;
    instance.truncate(10)?;
    let graph = instance.graph(8, Rounding::None)?;
    let problem = RoutingContext::set_partitioning(graph, instance.vehicles as f64)?;
    let mut options = TreeOptions::default();
    options.time_limit = Duration::from_secs(60);
    options.root.cg.multiplicity = (instance.customers.len() - 1) as f64;
    let result = tree::solve(problem, options)?;
    println!("{:?}: incumbent={}, bound={}",
        result.status, result.incumbent, result.bound);
    Ok(())
}
 The CLI tree uses Instance::vehicles as its fleet bound. Root-only CLI andInstance::solve use the customer count instead. A custom Rust callerchooses the bound in RoutingContext::set_partitioning and configures thecolumn-generation multiplicity separately.

Explicit fixed partial solutions The Rust API can fix additional integer quantities of physical elementary routesin a captured node: use baldes::master::routing::{self, FixedRoute};

let child = routing::inherit_fixed_routes(
    parent_snapshot,
    &[FixedRoute { arcs: route_arc_ids, quantity: 1 }],
    options,
)?;
 Quantities must be positive. Paths are reconstructed and checked against graphresources and the node's pricing constraints. Calls are cumulative: fixing moreroutes in a returned snapshot retains its existing fixed part. Over-covering acustomer or violating a later branch produces an infeasible residual node.Parents and siblings retain their original row bounds, basis and fixed state. Base and inherited active cut rows are shifted by each added route contribution.The fixed elementary part uses full-memory coefficients for rank-one and strongk-path rows; remaining LP columns can retain their valid weaker limited-memorycoefficients. Separation sees fixed routes alongside residual LP routes, and newcut RHS values are shifted once. This also handles capacity/complement and mappedresource-knapsack rows. Remaining fleet multiplicity and zero-arc propagationapply to the residual routes. LP objectives, Lagrangian bounds, incumbents and enumerated MIP outcomes includethe fixed cost. RootOutput::routes and values retain their residual-columnmeaning. fixed_routes and fixed_cost expose the fixed part;RootOutput::route_values() combines it with positive residual route values.Check solver status and artificial values before accepting an LP witness.Enumerated MIP route values already include fixed routes when a witness exists. A fresh fixed-route solve discards inherited cutoff-restricted enumeration andbucket fixing. It also starts with the caller's incumbent cutoff, because aparent incumbent need not satisfy the fixed restriction. Automatic enumerationand MIP gates retain their existing behavior; fixing routes does not force aninteger solve. The API does not choose routes to fix automatically, and currentlyhas no CLI or Python entry point. This single-network routing tree does not provide multiple pricing networks orcut callbacks during the integer solve. Python exposes routing root solving; useRust or the CLI for this tree. For a Python shared-master integer tree, usepyBaldes network models, whose supportedmodel and branching families differ. Infeasible means all nodes were closed without a feasibleincumbent, under the selected bound mode and solver tolerances. Optimal uses the configured absolute and relative gap tolerances. The reportedlower bound retains the minimum node bound across open and tolerance-closedsubproblems; closing a node does not replace its lower bound with the incumbent.The final bound can therefore remain below the incumbent after successful gapclosure. If the retained bound cannot justify closure, the status is Incomplete. Vehicle-count upper branches reuse the fleet row when its coefficients matchthe branch. Repeated bounds keep the tighter RHS, while branch depth stillcounts every recorded decision. Fixed route quantities reduce that RHS once.A custom fleet row with different coefficients receives a separate branch row. The root master receives explicit root.initial_routes. Child masters inherittheir parent's restricted model and generated column pool. Routes collectedglobally for integer search, including automatic singletons and routes fromother evaluations, do not preload the child's restricted model. Equal-boundchild insertion visits the upward branch before its downward sibling. For normal tree evaluations, a compatible preceding LP can supply numericalstate after its trailing branch columns and rows are removed. Exact orderedmatrix/cost/sense/offset checks precede transfer, and the target node keeps itsown row and column bounds. Other layouts retain the independent parent fork;strong trials and dives remain isolated. Stabilization centers belong to theparent, while a tightened vehicle upper bound sets the remaining routemultiplicity. Warm metadata retains the parent's numerical and certified lower boundsseparately. Stabilization gap gating compares the selected inherited boundwith the preceding artificial-free restricted-LP boundwithin each CG round. This differs from the global integer incumbent used forpruning and enumeration. Before mutating cuts, an artificial-free completed pricing round retains acheckpoint of the master, route registry, cut/pricing state, stabilization andfixing-gap cache. A later pricing timeout that explicitly requests saved-pointrollback restores that checkpoint and preserves any improved incumbent. Childnodes start with a fresh saved-point flag. Unsaved timeouts, label limits andother errors still propagate. Recovery uses the saved pricing bound; theunfinished cut round's restricted LP objective supplies no new certificate.Failed pricing attempts remain included in the recovered node's pricing-callcount and pricing time, while its bound and warm state come from the checkpoint.

Start with capacity and rank-one cuts cargo run --release --features clp --bin baldes -- \
  solve instances/Solomon/R101.txt --customers 25 --capacity-cuts --rank1 --time 60
 An experimental rank-one selection budget can limit how many active cuts touchany vertex through their support or memory: --rank1 --option BALDESrankOneCutsMaxPerVertex=8
 The default is 0 (disabled). Positive values count each cut once per vertexand graph, including both endpoints of memory arcs. Existing nonretired cutsconsume the budget across separation rounds and packing/covering passes.Candidates that exceed it are skipped in the separator's existing score order;lower-ranked candidates may fill the remaining slots. Existing cuts are retained,even if already over budget. This trades potential bound strength for cheaperpricing; 8 is an example to benchmark, not a tuned recommendation. The Rustregistry API exposes the same control as SeparationParameters::max_per_vertex. A complete Rust example: use baldes::{master::routing::RootOptions, inputs::{Instance, Rounding}};

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    let mut instance = Instance::read("instances/Solomon/R101.txt")?;
    instance.truncate(25)?;
    let rounding = Rounding::None;
    let capacity = instance.capacity_cut_config(rounding, false);
    let options = RootOptions::default()
        .with_capacity_cuts(capacity)
        .with_rank_one_cuts();
    let result = instance.solve(8, rounding, options)?;
    println!("{:?}: root LP={}", result.master.status, result.master.lp);
    Ok(())
}
 Use capacity_cut_config(rounding, true) to attach the instance's ready/duewindows, service times, and travel matrix for Kohl separation. The CLI/Python2-path switch is for VRPTW; CVRP inputs do not supply time windows. Capacity/Kohl separation has priority 3 at the root and 1 below it. It shares apersistent subset pool. Basic cut rows may be retired by cleanup; reported cutcounts can therefore exceed the number of rows still active.

Configure or disable A complete Rust example, requiring clp: use baldes::{master::routing::RootOptions, inputs::{Instance, Rounding}};

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    let mut instance = Instance::read("instances/Solomon/R101.txt")?;
    instance.truncate(10)?;
    let mut options = RootOptions::default();
    options.cg.smoothing.initial_alpha = 0.5;
    options.cg.smoothing.beta_factor = 0.0;
    options.cg.smoothing.max_updates = 10;
    let result = instance.solve(8, Rounding::None, options)?;
    println!("{:?}: root LP={}", result.master.status, result.master.lp);
    Ok(())
}
