Column elimination baldes::extensions::ce solves the continuous elementaryroute set-partitioning relaxation for CVRP and VRPTW. It uses a layereddecision diagram and an arc-flow LP, followed by infeasible-prefix refinement.It is an independent implementation inspired by Karahalios's referencesolver and the work of Karahaliosand van Hoeve, Column elimination for capacitated vehicle routing problems. The general initial diagram records the number of customer visits and thelast location, with at most n visits per route. It contains every feasibleelementary route but also permits non-elementary and resource-infeasibleroutes. Transitions that cannot meet the destination time window even fromthe source's earliest service time, and customers whose demand exceedscapacity, are excluded. Forward propagation then computes independent lowerbounds on accumulated load and service-start time. An arc is removed if eventhese optimistic bounds violate capacity or a time window. A reverse passpropagates latest feasible service times with conservative outward rounding,removes regions that cannot reach the end depot, and compaction physicallyreclaims dead nodes and arcs before the LP is built. These reductions alsoapply after each refinement batch and preserve feasible routes with directed,nonmetric travel times and zero-demand customers. For at least 20 customers with finite, nonnegative time windows, the solvercan instead index states by location and a rounded-down service-time bucket.The bucket width is a power of two bounded by the smallest customer transitionduration. Every transition must advance its bucket, preserving acyclicity.Anchors are lower bounds, so all feasible elementary routes remain represented;exact replay still checks original times, capacity and repeated visits.This mode is selected only when the estimated state count is at most onequarter of the layered construction and fits the budgets. Unsupported timeranges, zero-duration transitions or oversized graphs use the existing fallback.Identical relaxed suffixes are also merged across layers; topological orderingis recomputed after refinement rather than relying on the original visit count. For capacity-only instances with at least 20 customers and positive integerdemands, the solver can instead compile states indexed by load and location.Loads are divided by their demand GCD without rounding. Each location alsohas a two-customer ng neighborhood (itself and its cheapest reachableneighbor), retaining limited visit memory to prevent some repetitions early.Positive loads provide an acyclic ordering. This construction is selectedonly when every window has an infinite upper bound, capacity fits the boundedinteger representation, scaled capacity is at most 4n, and conservativestate/arc estimates fit the configured budgets. Otherwise the generallayered construction is used. Capacity states strengthen the initial boundbut can increase the latency of the first LP solve. The LP imposes customer visit equalities,flow conservation at each internal node, and an optional fleet upper bound.Depot-to-depot empty routes are excluded. After each optimal LP solve, the extension decomposes arc flows into pathsand replays capacity, service-start windows, waiting, service duration andelementarity. Each infeasible prefix is refined into exact states containingits last location, visited-customer set, load and service-start time. Equalstates share a node only when their active resource values match bit for bit.Time is omitted from exact-state keys only if all window upper bounds areinfinite and a conservative forward bound proves that time cannot overflowon an elementary route. Everyoutgoing transition from a new exact state is checked immediately, eliminatingall detectable conflicts rather than just the reported one. Incoming prefixeswith different resources retain their own successors.Prefixes are resolved against the updated diagram after each split so sharedprefixes and conflicts already removed by the batch are handled safely. TheLP is rebuilt after the batch, rather than after every individual conflict.Consequently every feasible route survives refinement. If the completedecomposition is feasible, it supplies a route-LP solution matching thearc-flow lower bound, establishing LP convergence within numerical tolerances. use baldes::extensions::ce::{Options, Problem, Status};
use baldes::master::solvers::ClpMaster;

let problem = Problem::from_instance(&instance)?;
